{"term":"s'accoupler","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Reflexive verb meaning to couple or mate; primarily used in animal biology but applied to humans with a mildly reductive or humorous connotation, foregrounding the mechanical and reproductive nature of the act.","english_term":"to mate / to couple","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Applied to humans it carries an animalistic undertone that can be comic or dismissive; the primary biological register makes it cruder in human contexts than its technical score suggests."}
{"term":"baiser","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The primary and most widely used crude French verb for 'to fuck'; originally meaning 'to kiss', the sexual sense has become so dominant in spoken French that 'baiser' is largely avoided in polite speech even with the original meaning.","english_term":"to fuck","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The semantic shift from 'to kiss' to 'to fuck' is so thorough that public speakers prefer 'embrasser' to avoid the crude reading; also used idiomatically as 'to screw over' (to cheat or deceive someone)."}
{"term":"niquer","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":86,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Highly crude verb meaning 'to fuck', borrowed into French from Arabic 'nâka' via North African immigrant communities and now widespread in urban and youth slang.","english_term":"to fuck","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Arabic loanword that entered metropolitan French via banlieue speech; extremely common in rap and online contexts; also means 'to break/destroy something' or 'to rip someone off', paralleling the polysemy of English 'fuck'."}
{"term":"fourrer","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":74,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang verb meaning 'to stuff inside' and by extension 'to fuck'; the primary meaning is to push or pack something into a container, repurposed as a penetration metaphor.","english_term":"to stuff / to screw","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Non-sexual uses are entirely standard ('fourrer dans sa poche', 'fourrer son nez partout'); the sexual sense is recognized by virtually all adult speakers and is active in everyday slang."}
{"term":"tringler","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang verb for having sex, derived from 'tringle' (a metal curtain rod or iron bar), evoking the rod-like shape of the penis as a penetration metaphor.","english_term":"to fuck / to rod","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Somewhat dated but still recognized; the curtain rod etymology is the standard derivation cited in French argot dictionaries; associated with 19th and early 20th century working-class slang."}
{"term":"tirer un coup","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_opportunistic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":94,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude idiom literally meaning 'to fire a shot', used to describe a casual or opportunistic sexual encounter, framing sex as a single physical discharge analogous to firing a weapon.","english_term":"to get laid / to get a quickie","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The firearm metaphor emphasizes tension-release and the transactional nature of the encounter; common across all age groups and social registers; can imply either mutual or unilateral casual sex."}
{"term":"sauter","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":73,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang verb meaning 'to jump', used metaphorically for 'to fuck'; typically transitive ('sauter quelqu'un'), with the 'jumping on' someone evoking physical aggression or eagerness.","english_term":"to fuck / to jump","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The primary non-sexual meaning 'to jump' is entirely standard; the sexual sense is ubiquitous in colloquial French and broadly understood; the aggression level is moderate compared to 'défoncer' or 'niquer'."}
{"term":"bourrer","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude verb meaning 'to stuff, pack, or cram tightly', used for penetrative sex with an aggressive filling connotation; implies force and fullness rather than mere entry.","english_term":"to stuff / to ram","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More graphically aggressive than 'fourrer' due to the cramming connotation; also appears in 'bourrer le crâne' (to fill someone's head with propaganda) and 'se bourrer la gueule' (to get drunk)."}
{"term":"ramoner","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":76,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang verb for having sex, borrowed from the verb 'ramoner' meaning 'to sweep a chimney'; the repeated up-down motion of a chimney brush inside a flue is the sexual metaphor.","english_term":"to fuck / to sweep","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the more visually inventive French sexual metaphors; still in active use though carrying a slightly retro or working-class flavor; the chimney sweep etymology is transparent to most speakers."}
{"term":"défoncer","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":91,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Very crude and aggressive verb literally meaning 'to smash through, bust open, or knock the bottom out of'; in sexual contexts implies maximally forceful penetration to the point of rupture.","english_term":"to destroy / to pound / to wreck","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the most graphically violent sexual verbs in current French slang; the smashing-through imagery implies complete obliteration of resistance; extremely common in online pornographic contexts and rap lyrics; also means to get very high on drugs or to wreck an object."}
{"term":"se taper","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":68,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Reflexive slang phrase literally meaning 'to hit or bang oneself against someone'; 'se taper quelqu'un' means to have sex with them, with a mildly objectifying connotation treating the partner as something encountered.","english_term":"to bang / to do","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also used non-sexually as 'se taper quelque chose' meaning to be stuck enduring something tedious ('se taper la vaisselle' = to be lumbered with the washing up); the sexual sense is inferred from context."}
{"term":"crosser","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":"QC","definition":"Crude verb meaning to have sex in metropolitan French; in Quebec French the dominant sense is 'to masturbate', while in France it can mean to have sex or to deceive someone, creating significant semantic divergence across varieties.","english_term":"to fuck / to jerk off","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Strong regional semantic split: Quebec French uses this almost exclusively for masturbation; in metropolitan France the sexual intercourse sense competes with 'to cheat/trick'; recognition is higher in Quebec than in France."}
{"term":"choper","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_success","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":50,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":89,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Slang verb primarily meaning 'to catch or grab'; in sexual contexts means to successfully pick someone up and have sex with them, foregrounding the seduction and acquisition of the partner.","english_term":"to score / to hook up with","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The primary meaning 'to catch' is dominant and neutral; the sexual sense is inferred from context; also used for catching a cold ('choper un rhume') or being arrested; cruder verbs describe the act itself once the 'getting' has succeeded."}
{"term":"chevaucher","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"cowgirl","concept":"cowgirl_position","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":40,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literary verb meaning 'to ride a horse', used as a sexual metaphor specifically for the on-top straddling position during intercourse, where the partner straddles and rides the other as one would a mount.","english_term":"to ride / to straddle","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More literary than crude in register; the equestrian metaphor appears in medieval and Renaissance French literature with clear sexual double meanings; understood by virtually all adult speakers but rarely the first lexical choice in crude speech."}
{"term":"culbuter","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":60,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":57,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dated crude verb literally meaning 'to knock over, topple, or tumble'; in sexual contexts means to throw a partner down and have sex with them, implying the receptive partner is physically overturned.","english_term":"to tumble / to knock down and take","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Carries a deliberately archaic or ironic flavor in contemporary speech; appears frequently in 18th and 19th century French erotic literature; Rabelais and subsequent libertine writers used it widely."}
{"term":"troncher","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":67,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang verb for having sex, probably derived from 'tronc' (trunk or torso) or 'tronche' (head/face), implying forceful full-body penetrative sex.","english_term":"to fuck","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Etymology disputed between 'tronc' (trunk, evoking the body as an object) and 'tronche' (face/mug, possibly via a sense of getting in someone's face); recognized across French slang registers though less ubiquitous than 'baiser' or 'niquer'."}
{"term":"besogner","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":63,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":76,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Old crude verb derived from 'besogne' (task or labor); means to have sex with someone in the sense of 'working on' them or performing one's function upon them, framing sex as mechanical labor.","english_term":"to do / to service","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The labor metaphor casts sex as a dutiful or joyless task; found in Rabelais and 16th–18th century libertine texts; still understood today but carries an unmistakably archaic or self-consciously retro tone."}
{"term":"se faire","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":63,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Reflexive slang phrase meaning 'to do to oneself'; 'se faire quelqu'un' means to have sex with them, objectifying the partner as something acted upon or consumed.","english_term":"to do someone / to have someone","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The reflexive construction 'se faire' is highly polysemous: 'se faire quelqu'un' can mean to have sex with, to beat up, or to rob; the sexual sense is strongly context-dependent; mildly more objectifying than 'coucher avec'."}
{"term":"renverser","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":55,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":63,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Old slang verb literally meaning 'to overturn or knock over'; in sexual contexts implies pushing a partner onto their back and having sex with them, with the 'reversal' suggesting the moment of positioning.","english_term":"to overturn / to lay over","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily encountered in 18th and 19th century French erotic texts; largely archaic in contemporary speech; recognition depends heavily on exposure to pre-20th century French literature or historical erotica."}
{"term":"monter","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Verb literally meaning 'to mount, climb, or go up'; in sexual contexts means to mount a partner for intercourse, directly paralleling the animal husbandry sense of a male mounting a female.","english_term":"to mount","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The animal husbandry register makes this subtly reductive when applied to humans; 'monter' is also a completely standard non-sexual verb (to go upstairs, to assemble, to ride a horse), so context is essential to parse the sexual sense."}
{"term":"gratter","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":50,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Slang verb literally meaning 'to scratch or scrape', used in some regional and dialectal registers of French to mean to have sex, evoking the rhythmic friction of the act through the scratching metaphor.","english_term":"to scratch / to fuck","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less widespread than most other entries on this list; the sexual sense is not universally recognized across all French-speaking regions; the scratching metaphor parallels English 'itch' idioms for sexual craving."}
{"term":"pourfendre","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":55,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":44,"recognition_pct_peak":54,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literary verb meaning 'to cleave through or split apart with a blade'; in sexual contexts, used in elevated or deliberately archaic registers to describe aggressive penetrative intercourse, evoking a sword bisecting an opponent.","english_term":"to cleave / to impale","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is swordsmanship — cutting an adversary in two — repurposed as a sexual metaphor in high-register erotic writing; rarely used in casual speech; recognition is largely confined to readers of classical French prose."}
{"term":"s'envoyer en l'air","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Common French euphemism literally meaning 'to send oneself into the air', used to mean to have sex, framing the act as a pleasurable, weightless escape or physical thrill.","english_term":"to get it on / to get laid","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the most widely recognized and used French euphemisms for sex; the 'air' metaphor implies levity, fun, and mutual pleasure rather than aggression; understood across all age groups and registers."}
